      "fact_error": true,
      "notes": "In 'Why not the others', the ketone line reads: 'C. Ketone: it has a C=O group and is made by oxidising an alkanol, not by adding water to an alkene.' This is stated as if any alkanol oxidises to a ketone. At syllabus depth (the alkanols subtopic itself teaches this), only a secondary alkanol oxidises to a ketone; a primary alkanol oxidises to an aldehyde and then a carboxylic acid, and a tertiary alkanol resists oxidation. 'an alkanol' is an unscoped generalisation of a claim that is only true for one class of alkanols, so it teaches the student something false about the other two classes.",
      "fix": "C. Ketone: it has a C=O group and is made by oxidising a secondary alkanol, not by adding water to an alkene.",
      "lane": "claude-sonnet"
    },
